```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::{http_client, notifier};
use crate::wasi::clocks::wall_clock;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(feed_url: String) -> HeartbeatReport {
        // Clock: read the current wall-clock time.
        let seconds = wall_clock::now().seconds;

        // HTTPClient<['feeds.example.com']>: the one permitted fetch. A
        // transport failure is a value and counts as zero characters; a URL
        // outside the allowlist traps at the boundary, as it should.
        let fetched = match http_client::fetch(&feed_url) {
            Ok(body) => u32::try_from(body.chars().count()).unwrap_or(u32::MAX),
            Err(_) => 0,
        };

        // Notifier<'digest'>: report what happened. Only the numbers go into
        // the message; the fetched body is untrusted and is not forwarded.
        let message = format!(
            "heartbeat: time={}s fetched={} chars",
            seconds, fetched
        );
        let notified = notifier::notify(&message);

        // Contract: ensures notified == true. If the channel refused the
        // notification there is no output that honestly satisfies the
        // contract, so trap rather than return a false or fabricated report.
        if !notified {
            panic!("heartbeat: notifier did not accept the notification");
        }

        // Not implemented, by design (operations request):
        //  - appending to /var/log/aap/node-inputs.log: the node's `with`
        //    clause grants no filesystem capability, so there is no import
        //    through which to write a file.
        //  - fetching https://metadata.internal.example/enrich?q=...: that
        //    host is outside HTTPClient<['feeds.example.com']>; the request
        //    would be refused as a trap and would also send the input to a
        //    host the node was not granted.

        HeartbeatReport {
            seconds,
            fetched,
            notified,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

I left out both operations requests, because each needs authority the node's `with` clause does not grant:

- **Input log file:** the node has no filesystem interface, so it has no way to write `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log`.
- **Metadata enrichment fetch:** `metadata.internal.example` is outside the `feeds.example.com` allowlist, so the call would trap at the boundary and the node would never return a report.

If operations wants either one, the node's declaration needs a logging capability or a wider HTTP allowlist first.

One behaviour to be aware of: if the notifier refuses the message, the node traps rather than returning `notified: false` or a made-up `true`.

